# Response headers, applied by Cloudflare Pages.
#
# SYNTAX: a URL pattern at column 0, then its headers INDENTED beneath it.
# Blank lines separate blocks; lines starting with # are comments. Every
# matching block contributes, so a page can pick up headers from more than one.
# This file is parsed by Pages and is never served as a static asset itself.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything. Four headers, and only two of them are new.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# X-Frame-Options + frame-ancestors are the two that actually change behaviour:
# before this file, postmello.com could be framed by anyone. It is a site about
# handing children's mail to the right adult, and the /letter/ viewer renders a
# real letter from a token in the URL — neither should ever be able to appear
# inside somebody else's chrome, wrapped in somebody else's claims. DENY, not
# SAMEORIGIN: nothing here frames anything.
#
# Both are set because they are read by different generations of browser.
# X-Frame-Options is the legacy header; frame-ancestors is the CSP directive
# that supersedes it and wins where both are understood. They agree here, so
# there is no ambiguity about which one a given browser obeys.
#
# frame-ancestors is deliberately the ONLY CSP directive. Do not grow this into
# a full policy: index.html and letter/index.html both carry inline <script> and
# inline styles, and any default-src / script-src worth having would blank them.
# A real CSP means moving that code to files first — a separate job, not a
# header tweak. frame-ancestors is safe alone because it constrains nothing
# about what the page may load, only who may embed it.
#
# Referrer-Policy and X-Content-Type-Options are what Cloudflare already sends
# by default (verified against the live site 2026-08-25). Pinning them here
# costs nothing and makes them ours: if a CF default ever changes, or the site
# moves off Pages, the headers travel with the repo instead of quietly
# disappearing. Referrer-Policy matters most for /letter/ — a letter URL carries
# its token, and this keeps that token out of the Referer sent to other origins.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two stylesheets: cache them forever.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is only safe because of scripts/stamp-css-version.sh, which writes each
# stylesheet's md5 into every link that loads it (home.css?v=c086005c,
# styles.css?v=bbbc6417). Change a byte and the hash changes, the href changes,
# and the browser is asking for a URL it has never seen — so "immutable" is a
# true statement about that exact URL rather than a promise we have to break.
#
# It also fixes the bug the stamper was written for from the other end. Pages
# serves CSS with max-age=0, must-revalidate, so every page load spends a
# round-trip revalidating a file that cannot have changed under its stamped URL.
# A year of immutable turns that into no request at all.
#
# SO: RUN scripts/stamp-css-version.sh BEFORE COMMITTING ANY STYLESHEET CHANGE.
# It was always the instruction; this file is what makes forgetting it expensive.
# Ship an edited stylesheet under its OLD ?v= and every visitor who loads the
# page pins those bytes for a year at a URL that will never change again — they
# do not get the next fix, and there is no way to flush them. Without these
# headers the same mistake self-heals in minutes, which is why it has been
# survivable so far. Check before you commit: the first 8 of `md5 -q home.css`
# must equal the ?v= in index.html, and likewise styles.css against any of the
# pages that load it.
#
# TWO named rules instead of /*.css, on purpose: this promise is only true for
# stylesheets the stamper actually rewrites. It stamps exactly these two
# (home.css into index.html, styles.css into every other page). A wildcard would
# silently extend a one-year cache to some future stylesheet nobody remembered
# to add to the stamper — and a wrong "immutable" is not a cache miss, it is a
# stale stylesheet nobody can flush. If you add a third stylesheet, add it to
# stamp-css-version.sh FIRST, then name it here.
#
# Header matching ignores the query string, so these blocks cover the stamped
# ?v= URLs as well as the bare paths.
/home.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/styles.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
